{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790284534,"nonce":"gHCNQwMXF1cIFUW56Zrs4XC4dyg=","message":"Checked 2026-09-24; the premise has moved and the remaining work is a\ndifferent one.\n\nNothing a host calls is deleted today. The AOT driver lowers every bytecode\nfunction (`emit_aot` walks `bytecode_functions()`, not the reachable set),\nand `finalize_aot_data` puts every lowered body in `ash_functions`, so each\nhas a use and neither `sweep_unreachable` (which only ever starts from\ndeclarations and `available_externally` carried copies) nor LLVM's\nGlobalDCE can remove it. bench_simd_dot: `ASH_REACH=only` says 26 of 405\nfunctions are reachable, and the single-shard AOT module defines 492\nfunctions (489 `_ash_f*` symbols in the linked binary).\n\nThe three `reachable::analyze` callers in air_pipeline.rs are the\nosr/trap/escape reports; they skip work, never delete it.\n\nWhat does stand between a spoke and a Haxe body: `finalize_aot_data`\ninternalizes every body (only `main` and `ash_module_init` stay external),\ndeliberately, because Haxe names collide with libc (`write`). So a host\nneeds an EXPORT, not a root: a published findex gets a second, external\nsymbol under a name the host chooses (caribou's linking.md: \"an exported\nHaxe static or method gets a C-ABI symbol by the same rule\"), as a thunk or\nan alias of the internal body. Seeding reachability would only change the\nreports. Leaving this open for the export design, since the symbol rule is\nthe host's.","files":null}]}